A||||7||tob||1984 fiero wont start int.
Z||000000||tob||07-20-99||10:42 PM||||I just purchased a 1984 2.5 fiero se.<br>i find after i drive the car for about 60 kms straight it will not start moments later .Lights come on battery registers at 13v according to the dash as i try turning the engine over, I jump start it and its fineagain until i make the same trip over again please help since it is embarrasing. By the the way there is no solenoid sound when turning car over<br>
Z||000001||batboy||07-20-99||10:58 PM||||Your problem could be one of many things, but I had a car once that wouldn't turn over when it was hot, but would start right up again if it cooled down. Turned out to be the starter going bad. Major auto parts stores usually will check them for you for free.
Z||000002||JetroGT||07-20-99||11:21 PM||||I used to own an 86 4cly 5spd that did the exact same thing. It turned out to be a bad starter. So I bought a reman from Track auto. That thing was junk. The housing at the gear cracked and broke off. After going back and getting my money back, I went to the junk yard and bought a used starter from a Chevey Bretta and I never had a problem with it after that.
Z||000003||ChadMan||07-21-99||07:14 AM||||My 84 did the same thing, tob.  It was a bad starter solenoid - no click, no nothing.  I replaced the starter and a year later it did the same thing.  I got another starter and nine months later, same story.  I took it to my mechanic, who noticed that the heat shield had rusted and fallen off my catalytic converter.  He guessed the heat was cooking the solenoids.  I made a heat shield out of galvenized metal and put yet another new starter on it and never had any more trouble.  (Of course if you live in a state without emissions tests, replacing your cat with a straight pipe might fix the heat problem too.)
Z||000004||theogre||07-21-99||07:53 AM||||don't forget to eliminate loose/coroded wires first. Wires are cheap, and for the most part easier to fix. (unless it's the end at the starter.....) 
Z||000005||Racer 1||07-22-99||04:25 AM||||Heres the fix get a JC Whitney catolog.Get the hot weather remote starter siliniod exact same as the older fords remote stater siliniod switch.This will cure your problem for only 22.00 plus tax or get a ford's from a autozone etc for real cheap.Before I found out about this remote starter I always had to push start my car was kinda embarassing when on date making my girl push the car while i ram it into second to get it started!!LOL oh the good old days GEEZ!!
Z||000006||PaUL||07-22-99||07:05 PM||||I suggested using a Ford type solenoid in another post but theOgre said it was unnecessary. I dont like the fact that the heavy gauge wire going to the starter is always live. I've accidently shorted this wire by touching it with a metal object many times. Once I touched it with the dipstick and it actually melted a small section of it. 
Z||000007||Racer 1||07-22-99||09:21 PM||||Paul I have sparked up to with the silinoid.But this was the fix for me .Before I found out about the remote siliniod I replaced 5 starters.Since I installed the rem siliniod I havent replaced one yet and its been about 4-5 years now!I always disconnect the power before doing any major work anyways!!
